# Decision
The application will use a Python HTTP server and SQLite to save vocabulary entries and their learning statuses.

# Context
The application needs to preserve vocabulary and learning progress when the user refreshes the page or closes and reopens the application. The walking skeleton must prove a real request from the browser through the backend to persistent storage and back, so SQLite provides a small structured store without introducing a separate database service.

# Alternatives Considered
SQLite - provides structured persistent storage in the repository with no separate database service
Browser Local Storage - simple, but does not prove the backend and storage layers or support server-side requests
JSON file - easy to understand, but less suitable for structured queries and concurrent requests

# Consequences
Using SQLite keeps the initial deployment small while proving the full request path and preserving vocabulary between sessions. The application remains single-user and does not automatically synchronize between different devices.